commit f40240b69a6d4643d7976ec7944b575ca587a801
Author: Piotr Bielak <pbielak@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri May 29 15:15:50 2026 +0200

    Rewrite `nonzero` kernel to use bounded scratch memory (#3374)
    
    For small tensors (N ≤ 2^25), keep the original single-pass
    `pstl::copy_if`
    path which is faster (~1.2x). For large tensors, switch to a two-pass
    chunked
    algorithm that bounds scratch memory at ~512 MB regardless of input
    size:
    
    Pass 1 – count: a work-group tree reduction counts nonzeros per
    2^25-element
      chunk and writes one partial sum per WG.
    
    Pass 2 – scatter: for each chunk, a 0/1 mask and its inclusive prefix
    sum are
    computed in scratch buffers capped at 2^25 elements.
    ScatterToOutKernelFunctor
    converts flat indices to per-dimension coordinates and writes them
    directly
      into the output tensor.
    
    The previous implementation allocated 3×N×8 bytes of scratch (mask +
    prefix-sum
    + flat-index output), exceeding 100 GB for a 4B-element tensor. The
    chunked
    path makes `test_nonzero_large` (2^31-1 × 2, int8) pass on low-VRAM
    hardware.
    
    Additional cleanups:
    - Merge bool template specializations using `if constexpr`
    - Replace manual ceiling division with `at::ceil_div()`
    - Remove INT_MAX element-count limitation from Nonzero.cpp
